Requirements:
Roles:
USER
ADMIN
Application Rules:
Every user must login before accessing the application.
The default landing page after login is /calendar.
Public Pages:
/login
/register
Protected Pages:
/calendar
/profile
Admin Only Pages:
/schedule-management
/user-management
Authorization Rules:
USER:
Can access all authenticated pages except:
/schedule-management
/user-management
ADMIN:
Can access all pages.
Authentication Rules:
Unauthenticated users may only access:
/login
/register
Unauthenticated users attempting to access any protected page must be redirected to /login.
Authenticated users visiting /login or /register must be redirected to /calendar.
Security Rules:
Use Argon2 for password hashing.
Use JWT access tokens.
Use Refresh Token Rotation.
Use HttpOnly Secure Cookies for refresh tokens.
Never store refresh tokens in localStorage.
Never store access tokens in localStorage unless explicitly required.
Validate all request inputs.
Use generic authentication error messages.
Implement backend authorization checks.
Implement frontend route guards.
Never trust frontend role data.
Verify JWT on every protected request.
Load permissions from authenticated user context.
Log authentication and authorization failures.
Reuse the existing users table.
Explain security decisions before coding.
Development Rules:
Before generating code:
Analyze the existing implementation.
Analyze the existing users table.
Analyze the current JWT implementation.
Explain the architecture.
Explain security considerations.
Provide an implementation plan.
Do not generate code immediately.
Wait for approval before modifying files.
TypeScript Rules
Use TypeScript strict mode.
Never use any type.
Never use @ts-ignore unless explicitly approved.
Prefer explicit types over implicit types.
Create interfaces or types for API requests and responses.
Type all React component props.
Type all Express request and response objects.
Type all custom hooks.
Type all service functions.
React Rules
Use functional components only.
Use hooks instead of class components.
Keep components small and reusable.
Avoid duplicate code.
Extract reusable logic into custom hooks.
Use React Router for navigation.
Use lazy loading where appropriate.
Show loading states for async operations.
Handle error states properly.
Express Rules
Use controller/service architecture.
Keep business logic out of route files.
Validate request input before reaching controllers.
Use centralized error handling.
Use middleware for authentication and authorization.
Never expose internal errors to API consumers.
Database Rules
Reuse existing database schema whenever possible.
Do not create duplicate tables.
Do not rename columns without approval.
Use parameterized queries.
Prevent SQL injection.
Add indexes only when necessary.
Explain schema changes before applying them.
Security Rules
Never store passwords in plaintext.
Use Argon2 for password hashing.
Never expose password hashes.
Never trust frontend authorization.
Validate all user input.
Sanitize user-controlled data.
Use generic authentication errors.
Verify JWT on every protected request.
Store secrets in environment variables.
Never hardcode secrets.
Never commit secrets to source control.
Code Quality Rules
Follow SOLID principles.
Follow DRY principles.
Avoid code duplication.
Prefer composition over inheritance.
Write maintainable code.
Write production-ready code only.
Explain breaking changes before implementation.
Explain security implications before implementation.
Show affected files before making modifications.
Refactoring Rules
Analyze existing code before refactoring.
Do not rewrite working code without justification.
Preserve backward compatibility whenever possible.
Explain risks before changing authentication logic.
Wait for approval before major architectural changes.